  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$  of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $6-2y>0$ hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(6-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-24{y}^{2}+36y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-48y+36,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-48y+36 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $3$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-48.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(1)=-24$ is negative, we conclude that $1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=1$ gives the maximum possible volume.",
